# Per-machine Claude Code settings. Holds PIXELLAB_SECRET, which .mcp.json expands into the
# PixelLab MCP server's Authorization header — the committed .mcp.json carries the variable name
# only, never the token.
.claude/settings.local.json

# Purchased tileset packs that nothing uses: not in an import.meta.glob, not in a .tmj's tilesets[],
# not a `from:` in derived-objects.manifest.js, and not named by content/tilesets/maps/planned-maps.js.
# Eight of them were sitting in apps/web/src/assets/tilesets/ at 108 MB and were parked here by the
# Phase 90 workflow audit rather than deleted. Git history keeps every one; restore a pack with
# `git checkout <commit> -- "apps/web/src/assets/tilesets/<pack>"`.
#
# Two consumers make a pack load-bearing even though nothing globs it, and both were nearly missed:
# a `from:` source for a derived sheet (or `npm run assets:pack-objects` can no longer rebuild what
# the maps draw), and planned-maps.js's forward slate, which tests/unit/tile-palettes.test.js asserts
# actually exists on disk. Check both before parking anything else here.

# Painted Chronotravel plate sources: ~2.5 MB 1672x941 PNGs, one per destination. The committed
# artefact is apps/web/src/assets/plates/*.webp, built from these by `npm run assets:build-plates`.
# They stay out of git for their size, and out of apps/web/src/assets/ so nothing can reference the
# 2.5 MB copy by accident.
art-source/

# Playwright visual baselines are captured on Windows and committed as *-chromium-win32.png.
# A run on macOS auto-creates a parallel *-chromium-darwin.png set; those are unreviewed
# by definition, and CLAUDE.md's ladder requires reviewing a baseline before accepting it.
tests/e2e/*-snapshots/*-chromium-darwin.png
